</div>


  <div class="pane">
    <div class="meta">Annotated config (inline filler descriptions)</div>
    <pre class="config" aria-label="annotated config">

Application configuration - instance .config

autogenerated by ops-bootstrap v1.4 on 2025-12-30

[app]


environment used by the app provisioning (used to toggle feature flags, metrics)

ENV = "production"


debug mode — disabled on production builds (keeps stack traces hidden)

DEBUG = False


application session/signing secret (placeholder)

SECRET_KEY = "qwertyuiopasdfghjklzxcvbnm123456"


Web / HTTP binding settings

[web]


listen address for the app (0.0.0.0 = all interfaces)

HOST = "0.0.0.0"


port exposed by the internal service (reverse proxy usually in front: nginx/haproxy)

PORT = 8080


ops contact for this service (internal address)

ADMIN_EMAIL = "ops-team@example.local"


Database connection configuration

[database]


DB engine driver used by the ORM / DB client

DB_ENGINE = "postgresql"


Host/IP of the database cluster (internal network)

DB_HOST = "10.10.10.50"


Native DB listener port

DB_PORT = 5432


Logical database name (schema separation for services)

DB_NAME = "production_db"


Service account used by the application

DB_USER = "svc_app"


Service password (rotated via secrets manager in production; this is a placeholder)

DB_PASSWORD = "Prod!Passw0rd_2025"


End of file — do not commit real secrets. If you find credentials here, contact ops-team@example.local

    </pre>
  </div>
</div>

<section style="margin-top:18px" class="pane">
  <div class="meta">Field-by-field filler descriptions (for realism)</div>
  <dl>
    <dt>[app].ENV</dt>
    <dd>Defines the runtime environment for the application. Typical values: "development", "staging", "production". This toggles feature flags, logging verbosity, and which external backends are used.</dd>

    <dt>[app].DEBUG</dt>
    <dd>When true, the framework prints stack traces and enables auto-reload. Set to False in production to avoid leaking internals to end-users or attackers.</dd>

    <dt>[app].SECRET_KEY</dt>
    <dd>Used by the framework for cryptographic signing (session cookies, CSRF tokens). In real deployments this value is supplied by a secrets manager (Vault/SSM) and rotated regularly. Here it is a placeholder for developer convenience.</dd>

    <dt>[web].HOST</dt>
    <dd>Local bind address. Most deployments bind 127.0.0.1 and use a fronting proxy; a 0.0.0.0 bind is common in containerized setups so the container can accept traffic on the published port.</dd>

    <dt>[web].PORT</dt>
    <dd>The port the application listens on. This should match the container/external port mapping and health-check configuration used by orchestration.</dd>

    <dt>[web].ADMIN_EMAIL</dt>
    <dd>Primary oncall or owner contact for this service. Used for automated alerts (when configured) and as a quick reference for incident responders.</dd>

    <dt>[database].DB_ENGINE</dt>
    <dd>Selects the database adapter. "postgresql" indicates the app uses a Postgres-compatible client/ORM.</dd>

    <dt>[database].DB_HOST</dt>
    <dd>IP or DNS name of the database server or load-balancer on the internal network. In production this would typically be a private IP or a DNS name pointing to an RDS/DB cluster.</dd>

    <dt>[database].DB_PORT</dt>
    <dd>The network port the database listens on. Default Postgres port is 5432; some environments use a non-standard port for isolation.</dd>

    <dt>[database].DB_NAME</dt>
    <dd>The logical database/schema name the application connects to. Separate databases per service helps reduce blast radius and simplify DB-level RBAC.</dd>

    <dt>[database].DB_USER</dt>
    <dd>Service account the application uses for DB connections. This account should have least-privilege grants (only the required schemas/tables).</dd>

    <dt>[database].DB_PASSWORD</dt>
    <dd>Service account credential. In mature environments this is sourced from a secrets manager and not stored on disk; the presence of a static password is common in quick-testing or legacy deployments.</dd>
  </dl>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:10px">
    Deployment notes: This HTML is intended to be used as a decoy / documentation page accompanying an instance .config file. For honest operations, never store plaintext secrets in repo-accessible files; instead, use a secrets management workflow and restrict file permissions. If this page is used in a honeypot, the descriptive text increases believability for attackers while still keeping the values obviously test/non-production.
  </div>
</section>

